## Analysis

The problem is a **well-known Firefox bug** — `<button>` elements used as flex containers (`display: flex; align-items: center`) don't reliably vertically center their children in Firefox (including Firefox Android). This happens because Firefox applies internal button layout (default padding, baseline alignment) that overrides or conflicts with the flexbox behavior.

The navbar buttons at `index.html:201-212` use exactly this pattern — `<button className="nav-item">` with `display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;` wrapping an `<Icon>` SVG.

## Plan

Two options, from simplest to most robust:

### Option A (CSS-only, minimal risk)
Add `line-height: 0` to `.nav-item`. This strips the text baseline that Firefox uses internally in buttons, allowing the flex centering to work:

```css
.nav-item {
  ...
  line-height: 0;
}
```

**Pro**: 1-line CSS change.  
**Con**: May not fully resolve it on all Firefox Android versions.

### Option B (wrapper element, most reliable)
Wrap the icon in a `<span>` inside the button, so the `<span>` (not the `<button>`) handles flex centering:

**CSS** — Replace `.nav-item` flex properties with a new selector:
```css
.nav-item {
  flex: 1;
  border: none;
  background: none;
  color: var(--text3);
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 0;
  transition: color 0.15s;
}
.nav-item-icon {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}
```

**JSX** — In the `AppShell` component (~line 5884), wrap `<Icon>`:
```jsx
<button ...>
  <span className="nav-item-icon">
    <Icon name={item.icon} size={24} />
  </span>
</button>
```

**Pro**: Standard workaround tested across all Firefox versions.  
**Con**: Touches both CSS and JSX.

### My recommendation
Go with **Option B** — it's the battle-tested fix for this exact Firefox quirk and won't break any other browser. Firefox's `<button>`-as-flex-container issue has been unresolved for years, so the wrapper approach is the safest bet.

